near/14062337/assets/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,2\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-9296/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51f9b53-0ce5-518f-8173-abe4d87f2851"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9296"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-316598"] :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/6805467a24cb40559f205cbfcecc4f5a34ff2ec6ebda8ae67f997c02f123b53c.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-4)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1=-12-2=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-14\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-316606"] :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/3e2c56766f201095bee0eea101b877e30910c676c998f37d261fd7cf7aabb837.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-6\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-275983"] :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/a4e507191aecd02e16fafba29598972df423e975dd554b260ca355f0cf6476a1.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4=8.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-2)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,6\\,|\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7454"] :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that is given by its standard matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/4dbe8f14fe2189749e753080658086e4b7f608a5b7028151d2464b99be4a29ed.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5470"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/e8ff769df4089e6ed9a47d56d7317acf41b8aef6a255abc705c0e7ec31a446ea.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/a274b413d20b9aada9f32bc789557097df1a643805c2e8b12c389e3832352c95.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5471"] :question/problem "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1)$, $(4,5),$ and $(-2,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/5cb4d5976a3e7153d7041711ab449a1f3af48000021be1d1c4a2c321cbec3467.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062337/assets/ee8f0cf16983d87fa505303ed6372a3f389518d05b3e13f2e9e2121038792b84.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = (-7) \\cdot 3 -  5 \\cdot (-2) = -11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-11|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
